    "media:imagetext": "viting with a nasty \"sweet\" of cerise jello smeared neatly on top with a bit of whipped\ncream, the wholeshivering mess encased in a greasy paper cup with thumb marks. It was a\nmarked contrast to the svelte elegance of the President Special, and unlike the latter\nawfully easy to diet.\nArrived Hamburg in a heavy rain Saturday afternoon. Went to bed early that\nnight but couldn't sleep for four or five hours and woke up Sunday at what I thought\nwas 7:00 A.M. but turned out to be 1:00 P.M. which, after all, considering the six\nhours difference in time was, in fact, 7800 A.M., New Iork time. I Just can't make my\nold carcass conform to the clock!\nMy host and his wife took me for a drive out toward the sea and a walk along\nthe Oster River which formsthe estuary for the harbor. Many signs of the terrific\nbombing of Hamburg are evident at every turn, but the improvised shipyards were all\nbusy, even on a Sunday afternoon. The Germans are working. The labor leaders say in\neffect, \"Boys, we got licked - all me can do now 1s work.\"\nMonday we caught a train at 7:00 A.M. for the Journey down to Dusseldorf -\nmy host was old fashioned - he didn't like to fly - where me arrived at 1:00 P.M. I\nmust confess that I was just as scared as he would have been in the air, forthe train\nhit 90 miles per hour several times. That's too fast for me - when \"the milestones\n1ook 11ke a picket fence.\" Then, too, I couldn't see much of the country but when\nwe got down toward the industrial cities of the Ruhr the train slowed down to a decent\npace. All the cities along the railroad - especially Essen - still showed large tracts\nof wrecked buildings despite the new ones going up everywhere.\nAt Dusseldorf we stayed at the Briedenbacher Hog, a brand new, very elegant\nhotel erected where an old one had been completely wrecked by bombs. Tuesday I\ndrove over to Cologne with the Pan American No. One at Dusseldorf, Gunter Mayer-.\nRolshoven, who as you might suspedt is German, for a call on the reorganized Luft\nHansa. They are realy in trouble. 'They have airplanes ordered ready for delivery\nbut until something is done about the peace treaty they cant fly. They're all\ndressed up and have nowhere to go!\nOn Wednesday I drove to Wiesbaden - then to Frankfurt to talk to thepa",
